            "heading_mobilealignment": "center",
            "heading_color": "#ffffff"
          }
        },
        "text_BnFjzQ": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>As estrogen and androgen levels shift during perimenopause, they affect the glands that keep your tear film stable — particularly the meibomian glands, which produce the oily layer that stops tears from evaporating too fast. When that layer breaks down, eyes can feel gritty, tired, and dry, even if they look perfectly normal.</p><p>It's a real, hormone-driven change — not something you're imagining, and not something you just have to live with.</p>",
            "text_style": "subtitle",
            "text_alignment": "left",
            "text_mobilealignment": "left",
            "text_color": "#eaf6f3"
          }
        },
